<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE precept [
    <!ENTITY date "2026-07-02">
]>


<section xml:id="sec-hidden-order" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Lens of Representation: Uncovering Hidden Order</title>
  <introduction>
        Many of the most profound ideas in science are rooted in taking on new perspectives to think about
        physical systems that already have a well-established conventional representation. The <alert>big ideas</alert>
        do not emerge from changes in the physical system themselves (nor the methods used to observe and measure them), but rather
        newfound understandings that reveal hidden structures in nature.
  </introduction>

  <remark xml:id="boltzmann-transitions">
    <title>Before and After Boltzmann: Mysterious Variable-to-Combinatoric Counting-to-Clarity</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Before Boltzmann and his Second Law of Thermodynamics, <alert>entropy</alert> was an esoteric-like thermodynamic quantity <m>S</m>.
        Boltzmann was able to frame this headache of a physical quantity as a combinatorial statement rooted in basic counting (see
        <xref ref="def-boltzmann-entropy"/> for details on the Boltzmann entropy formulation).
      </p>
    </statement>



  </remark>
</section>